
The guard is registered against the built-in tool task, not a lookalike
  ok   a hook is registered on the "pi.tool" task
  ok   the handler the hook runs is this module's beforeTool
  ok   the extension is named, so a collision would be reported rather than silent

Commands that leave the boundary are blocked, with the reason as the block text
  ok   blocked: wsl ls
  ok   blocked: docker ps
  ok   blocked: docker-compose up
  ok   blocked: git reset --hard HEAD~1
  ok   blocked: git clean -fd
  ok   blocked: git checkout -- .

Ordinary work is not blocked — a guard that blocks everything is an outage
  ok   allowed: node demo/cli.test.mjs
  ok   allowed: git status
  ok   allowed: git diff HEAD
  ok   allowed: npm test
  ok   allowed: rg wanted src

Only shell tools are inspected, and nested calls are covered too
  ok   a non-shell tool is not inspected (its `path` is not a command)
  ok   a nested call — how a codemode script reaches a tool — is blocked too
  ok   a shell call with no command string is left alone rather than guessed at

One policy serves both layers (two lists would drift, and the drift would be a hole)
  ok   the environment and the guard give the same reason for the same command

Publishing is refused unless the OPERATOR granted it, which a seat cannot forge
  ok   git push is refused by default
  ok   an inline assignment in the seat's own command does NOT grant it
  ok   the operator's grant, set in durig's environment, allows it
  ok   the grant does not widen any other rule
  ok   and the refusal returns once the grant is gone

PASS — the guard holds

